A||||10||Seanh||Just got a 305 V8 w/broken Cam||||6||
Z||000000||Seanh||12-07-2000||06:55 PM||||Well, a friend of mine at school has an 82 Z-28 Camaro w/305 Engine. They were doing some "Excessive" driving the other night and broke a timing chain and probally the cam its self. He wants to ditch the engine and put in a 351, so me being me, told him I would take it off his hands. So, I am going to be getting a 305 V8 with a broken cam, and am wondering how to go about fixing it. Once I look at it, I will probally just rebuild the whole thing, top to bottem.  But if I decide not to, how should I remove the cam? Also, what level of Archies V8 kit would I need to install this? I probally woudnt use the master build kit, but should I use the mid priced one, or the lowest priced one? Thanks<BR>BTW, this will be going into my non-running black 85, once I am done with the 86GT.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||6
Z||000001||hugh||12-07-2000||07:18 PM||||1st,If the timing chain broke the engine may have bent valves,dinged pistons,bent pushrods,etc.<BR>2nd,a 305 is not a good engine to build,but would be a good engine to take apart and learn from.If you want to build an engine,don't use any less than a 350.You may be able to find a 4 bolt main engine in a junk yard.That would give you a solid foundation on which to build a killer engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||12-07-2000||09:27 PM||||Ditto what Hugh said and this too:<BR>Unless the car was fuel injected, it is not a HO and it has wimpy little valves and low flowing heads. It will be a good learning experience, but because the weight of a 305 is the same as pretty much every small block (give or take), I would hold out for a 350. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||12-07-2000||09:44 PM||||Oh well, I might just give it a try, probally just for the learning experience. The engine has Crossfire injection, TBI. The only reason I took this is because it was free. You really think I should just wait for a 350? Is there anything that could be done to this engine, like some better valves, to make it worth my time to put into the Fiero?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||12-08-2000||07:36 AM||||You say it is a crossfire engine.I believe that is the one with two throttle bodies.I have one I bought many years ago because it looked so good and was going to use it on a 305 engine I had in a 1950 Ford coupe(I bought it that way)That manifold doesn't flow air good enough to even be considered mediocre.If you think about it,there is a 45 cubic inch difference between the engines.That can equate to as much as 75 horsepower or much more in an engine that will cost no more to build. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||12-08-2000||02:35 PM||||Nobody likes the Crossfire injection, but it's not all that bad. It's not the best from a performance standpoint, but IMO it's better than any carb, and you won't find a TPI motor for free. The same system was used on the Corvette 350 in '82 and '84. There are a few minor improvements that can be done to make it work even better. I just finished installing a Crossfire system on the 305 in my Jeep (had a carb). It made a HUGE improvement in throttle response and power. I can see I'll be breaking even more axles now!! I believe the 82 305 CFI is rated 185 hp. It has the same valves as a 305 or 350 TPI, the TPI is just a more efficient system, and efficiency in a FI system is free power.<P> If it's free, it's for me!!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000006||Cooter||12-08-2000||03:38 PM||||Hmm. Then I wonder why my 84 carb. HO was rated at 190 HP. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wannabe||12-08-2000||07:27 PM||||The cross fire engine was 165hp. All of the early to mid '80s 305 were lightweight designs that make very poor rebuild prospects. There's very little material in the blocks and heads, so they will only tolerate minimal machining. Valve guides were prone to wear, and the blocks would distort easily if too heavily modified (kind of like the Duke)I think there was a recall for broken camshafts too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||12-08-2000||08:01 PM||||Airflow capabilities of the crossfire are extremely limited in comparison to a good aftermarket manifold.The small ports make for good mixture velocity and throttle response,but poor top end power.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fieroboy1||12-08-2000||09:40 PM||||You don't have to abandon the 305, you can make lots of hp from one.  I would ditch the whole crossfire and put in a nice edelbrock or comprable dual plane intake.  You can swap for higher flowing heads...etc.  I had an 83 camaro with a 305, i ditched the computer controlled carburator and went to a solid lifter cam i happened to have.  Man that motor was a screamer, it would rev to 6000 no problem.  Sure a 350 will make more power, but you can extract lots of ponies from the little mouse motors.||||reg||1
Z||000010||hugh||12-08-2000||10:01 PM||||Confusing isn't it?<BR>Once I was given a car,worst thing I could have done was to take it.That car needed an engine and I did it.By the time I got done I had more in the car than it was worth.What I am trying to say is,just because you got it for free doesn't mean it will be cheap.As I said in my first post use the engine as a learning tool.Take it apart slowly,turn it over by hand as you do and see what you can learn.The cost to build a 305 or a 350 will be almost exactly the same.Why go with an engine that will produce less horsepower?||||reg||1
